/* ---------- 2.1.2b Consideration and promissory estoppel ---------- */
TOPICS.push({
  id: '2.1.2b', unit: 'CT', ref: '2.1.2', title: 'Consideration and promissory estoppel',
  short: 'Definition, sufficiency and adequacy, past consideration, existing duties, part payment, promissory estoppel',
  summary: 'English law only enforces promises that are part of a bargain: each side must give something of value — consideration. The rules decide what counts: value but not necessarily adequate value; not past consideration; not merely performing an existing duty (unless there is a practical benefit); and not part payment of a debt. Promissory estoppel, from equity, softens the part-payment rule.',
  spec: ['Definition of consideration: Currie v Misa, Dunlop v Selfridge; executed and executory', 'Consideration must be sufficient but need not be adequate', 'Past consideration and its exceptions', 'Performance of an existing duty: public duty, contractual duty owed to the promisor (Stilk, Hartley, Williams v Roffey), duty owed to a third party', 'Part payment of a debt: Pinnel’s Case and Foakes v Beer, and exceptions', 'Promissory estoppel: High Trees and its requirements'],
  learn: [
    { h: 'What is consideration?', html: `
<p>Consideration is what each party gives or promises in return for the other’s promise: the “<b>price</b> for which the promise of the other is bought” ([[c:dunlopselfridge]]). The classic definition is in [[c:currie]]: a benefit to one party or a detriment to the other.</p>
<ul><li><b>Executory</b> consideration: a promise to do something in the future (“I will pay you on delivery”).</li><li><b>Executed</b> consideration: an act already performed in exchange for a promise (e.g. finding the lost dog in a unilateral contract).</li></ul>
<p>Consideration must <b>move from the promisee</b> — the person enforcing the promise must have provided it ([[c:tweddle]]).</p>` },
    { h: 'Sufficient but not adequate', html: `
<p>Consideration must be <b>sufficient</b> — something the law recognises as having some value — but need not be <b>adequate</b> (a fair price). The courts do not judge bargains.</p>
<ul><li>[[c:thomasthomas]]: £1 a year rent was enough.</li><li>[[c:chappell]]: chocolate wrappers were part of the consideration.</li><li>[[c:whitebluett]]: a son’s promise to stop complaining had no value.</li><li>Natural love and affection is not consideration.</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Past consideration', html: `
<p>Consideration must be given in return for the promise; something already done before the promise is <b>past consideration</b> and is not good consideration ([[c:mcardle]]; <i>Roscorla v Thomas</i>, 1842).</p>
<div class="box tip"><b class="lbl">Exception</b><p>[[c:lampleigh]], refined in [[c:paoon]]: past consideration is good if (1) the act was done at the promisor’s <b>request</b>; (2) both parties understood it would be <b>paid for</b>; and (3) the payment would have been legally enforceable had it been promised in advance. Example: asking a plumber to fix a leak and afterwards promising £100.</p></div>` },
    { h: 'Performing an existing duty', html: `
<ul><li><b>Existing public duty</b>: not good consideration ([[c:collins]]), but doing <b>more</b> than the duty is ([[c:glasbrook]]).</li>
<li><b>Existing contractual duty owed to the promisor</b>: traditionally not good consideration ([[c:stilk]]), unless the promisee does more than originally required ([[c:hartley]]).</li>
<li>[[c:roffey]] created an important exception: a promise to pay more for the same work is enforceable if the promisor obtains a <b>practical benefit</b> (e.g. avoiding a penalty clause, avoiding the trouble and cost of finding a replacement) and the promise was not obtained by fraud or <b>economic duress</b>.</li>
<li><b>Existing contractual duty owed to a third party</b>: good consideration (<i>Shadwell v Shadwell</i>, 1860; <i>Scotson v Pegg</i>, 1861).</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Part payment of a debt', html: `
<p>The rule in <b>Pinnel’s Case</b> (1602), confirmed in [[c:foakes]]: payment of a smaller sum on the due date is not consideration for a promise to accept it in full settlement. The creditor can later claim the balance. [[c:selectmove]] held that Williams v Roffey’s practical benefit does not apply to part payment; in [[c:mwb]] the Supreme Court left the rule in place.</p>
<p><b>Exceptions:</b> payment early, at a different place (at the creditor’s request), or with something different in kind (“a horse, hawk or robe” — Pinnel’s Case); payment by a third party; composition agreements with creditors; and <b>promissory estoppel</b>.</p>` },
    { h: 'Promissory estoppel', html: `
<p><b>Promissory estoppel</b> is an equitable doctrine: where one party promises not to enforce their strict legal rights, and the other relies on that promise, the promisor may be <b>prevented (estopped)</b> from going back on it, even without consideration. Based on [[c:hughesmetro]], developed by Denning J in [[c:hightrees]].</p>
<ul><li>There must be an existing <b>legal relationship</b>.</li><li>A clear and unequivocal <b>promise</b> not to enforce strict rights.</li><li><b>Reliance</b> on the promise by the promisee.</li><li>It must be <b>inequitable</b> for the promisor to go back on it — the promisee must have acted fairly ([[c:dcbuilders]]).</li><li>It is a <b>shield, not a sword</b> ([[c:combe]]).</li><li>It is often <b>suspensory</b>: the promisor may be able to reinstate rights for the future with reasonable notice (<i>Tool Metal Manufacturing v Tungsten Electric</i>, 1955), though past arrears may be extinguished (High Trees).</li></ul>` }
  ],
  debate: [{ q: 'Should Foakes v Beer be overruled?', for: ['Creditors often gain a practical benefit from part payment (cash now, avoiding insolvency) — inconsistent with Williams v Roffey.', 'Businesses routinely agree to accept less; the law should reflect commercial reality.', 'Lord Blackburn in Foakes itself had doubts.', 'Promissory estoppel is uncertain as a substitute.'], ag: ['Protects creditors from pressure to accept less (D & C Builders).', 'Certainty: the Supreme Court declined to reconsider it in MWB (2018).', 'Only the Supreme Court can overrule it — CA bound (Selectmove).', 'Economic duress rules may be a better tool than changing consideration.'] }],
  cases: ['currie', 'dunlopselfridge', 'thomasthomas', 'chappell', 'whitebluett', 'mcardle', 'lampleigh', 'paoon', 'collins', 'glasbrook', 'stilk', 'hartley', 'roffey', 'foakes', 'dcbuilders', 'selectmove', 'mwb', 'hightrees', 'combe', 'hughesmetro'],
  worked: [{ q: '<b>Scenario.</b> Jay agreed to rebuild Kim’s wall for £2,000 by 1 May. Half-way through, Jay says he is losing money and will stop unless Kim pays £500 more. Kim, who is hosting a wedding in her garden on 3 May, agrees. Jay finishes on time. Kim now refuses to pay the extra £500. Advise Jay.', s: ['<b class="st">Issue</b>Is Jay’s performance of his existing contractual duty good consideration for Kim’s promise to pay more?', '<b class="st">Rule</b>Stilk v Myrick: performing an existing duty owed to the promisor is not consideration. But Williams v Roffey: it can be, if the promisor gains a practical benefit and there is no economic duress.', '<b class="st">Apply</b>Kim gains a practical benefit: the wall is finished before the wedding, and she avoids the cost and delay of finding another builder.', '<b class="st">Duress?</b>Jay threatened to stop work — was this illegitimate pressure (Atlas Express v Kafco)? He seems genuinely in difficulty and asked rather than exploiting Kim’s position — similar to Roffey, where the extra payment was offered. If it was economic duress, Kim’s promise would be voidable.', '<b class="st">Conclude</b>Jay can probably enforce the promise for £500 under Williams v Roffey, unless Kim can show economic duress.'], a: 'Likely enforceable — practical benefit (Williams v Roffey).' }],
  pitfalls: ['Saying consideration must be adequate or equal in value.', 'Applying Williams v Roffey to part payment of a debt — Re Selectmove says it does not apply.', 'Using promissory estoppel as a cause of action — it is a shield (Combe v Combe).', 'Forgetting the conditions in Pao On for past consideration.'],
  cards: [
    ['Currie v Misa definition?', 'A benefit to one party or a detriment to the other.'],
    ['Consideration must be sufficient but need not be ___?', 'Adequate.'],
    ['Thomas v Thomas (1842)?', '£1 a year was sufficient consideration.'],
    ['Chappell v Nestlé (1960)?', 'Chocolate wrappers were part of the consideration.'],
    ['Re McArdle (1951)?', 'Past consideration is no consideration.'],
    ['Pao On conditions for past consideration?', 'Done at promisor’s request; payment understood; would be enforceable if promised in advance.'],
    ['Stilk v Myrick (1809)?', 'Performing an existing contractual duty is not consideration.'],
    ['Williams v Roffey (1991)?', 'Practical benefit to the promisor can be consideration, absent duress.'],
    ['Foakes v Beer (1884)?', 'Part payment of a debt is not consideration for forgiving the rest.'],
    ['Exceptions to Pinnel’s Case?', 'Early payment, different place, something different in kind, third-party payment, promissory estoppel.'],
    ['Requirements of promissory estoppel?', 'Existing relationship; clear promise; reliance; inequitable to go back.'],
    ['Combe v Combe (1951)?', 'Promissory estoppel is a shield, not a sword.'],
    ['D & C Builders v Rees (1966)?', 'No estoppel where the promisee acted inequitably.'],
    ['Glasbrook Bros v Glamorgan CC (1925)?', 'Exceeding a public duty is good consideration.']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'In Thomas v Thomas, rent of £1 a year was held to be…', o: ['sufficient consideration', 'inadequate so no contract', 'past consideration', 'a gift'], x: 'Need not be adequate.' },
    { q: 'Which case is an exception to the rule on past consideration?', o: ['Lampleigh v Braithwait', 'Re McArdle', 'Roscorla v Thomas', 'Foakes v Beer'], x: 'Act done at the promisor’s request.' },
    { q: 'Williams v Roffey introduced the idea of…', o: ['practical benefit', 'promissory estoppel', 'the postal rule', 'the mirror image rule'], x: 'Avoiding the penalty clause.' },
    { q: 'Re Selectmove held that practical benefit does not apply to…', o: ['part payment of a debt', 'contracts for services', 'unilateral contracts', 'sale of goods'], x: 'Foakes v Beer still applies.' },
    { q: 'In Hartley v Ponsonby, extra pay was enforceable because the sailors…', o: ['did more than their existing contractual duty', 'had not yet signed on', 'were owed a public duty', 'gave past consideration'], x: 'The ship was dangerously undermanned.' },
    { q: 'Promissory estoppel cannot be used…', o: ['as a cause of action (a sword)', 'as a defence', 'where a promise was relied upon', 'in a landlord and tenant case'], x: 'Combe v Combe.' },
    { q: 'In D & C Builders v Rees, promissory estoppel failed because…', o: ['the Reeses had taken advantage of the builders’ difficulties', 'there was no promise', 'the debt was too small', 'the builders were a company'], x: 'He who comes to equity must come with clean hands.' },
    { q: 'Which is NOT an exception to the rule in Pinnel’s Case?', o: ['Paying a smaller sum late', 'Paying early at the creditor’s request', 'Giving something different in kind', 'Payment by a third party'], x: 'Late part payment is no exception.' },
    { q: 'Performing an existing public duty is…', o: ['not good consideration unless you do more', 'always good consideration', 'executed consideration', 'past consideration'], x: 'Collins v Godefroy; Glasbrook.' },
    { q: 'In Chappell v Nestlé, chocolate wrappers were…', o: ['part of the consideration', 'too trivial to count', 'past consideration', 'an invitation to treat'], x: 'Value to Nestlé in increasing sales.' }
  ],
  exam: [
    { q: 'Explain the rules on performance of existing duties as consideration. [10]', m: 10, ms: ['public duty — Collins v Godefroy', 'exceeding public duty — Glasbrook', 'contractual duty to promisor — Stilk v Myrick', 'exceeding — Hartley v Ponsonby', 'practical benefit — Williams v Roffey; conditions', 'economic duress limit', 'duty to third party — Shadwell / Scotson v Pegg', 'part payment distinction — Foakes, Selectmove'] },
    { q: 'Analyse and evaluate the rules on part payment of debts. [25]', m: 25, ms: ['Pinnel’s Case and Foakes v Beer', 'exceptions', 'promissory estoppel — High Trees, requirements', 'limits — D & C Builders, Combe, suspensory', 'Williams v Roffey inconsistency; Selectmove', 'MWB v Rock — Supreme Court’s approach', 'commercial reality vs protecting creditors', 'certainty vs justice', 'reform options', 'conclusion'] }
  ],
  tools: ['considertree']
});

/* ---------- 2.1.2c Intention and privity ---------- */
TOPICS.push({
  id: '2.1.2c', unit: 'CT', ref: '2.1.2', title: 'Intention to create legal relations and privity',
  short: 'Social and domestic presumption, commercial presumption; privity, exceptions, Dunlop v Lambert, the 1999 Act',
  summary: 'An agreement is only a contract if the parties intended it to be legally binding. The courts use presumptions: family and social arrangements are presumed not binding, commercial deals are presumed binding — both can be rebutted. Privity of contract means only the parties can enforce a contract, but there are important exceptions, especially the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999.',
  spec: ['Intention to create legal relations: social and domestic arrangements; rebutting the presumption', 'Commercial and business agreements; honour clauses; comfort letters', 'Privity of contract: the basic rule', 'Exceptions to privity, including Dunlop v Lambert, agency, trusts, collateral contracts and statute', 'The Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999'],
  learn: [
    { h: 'Social and domestic agreements', html: `
<p>Agreements between family members and friends are <b>presumed not to be intended to be legally binding</b>.</p>
<ul><li>Spouses living together: [[c:balfour]]. But if they have separated or are about to, the presumption is rebutted: [[c:merritt]].</li>
<li>Parents and children: [[c:jones]].</li>
<li>The presumption can be rebutted by evidence of serious intention: money changing hands or shared contributions ([[c:simpkins]]), major reliance and written terms ([[c:parkerclark]]).</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Commercial agreements', html: `
<p>In business agreements there is a <b>strong presumption</b> that the parties intend to be legally bound ([[c:edwards]]). The burden of rebutting it is heavy.</p>
<ul><li>An express <b>honour clause</b> can rebut it: [[c:rosefrank]].</li>
<li><b>Letters of comfort</b> (statements of present policy) may not be promises: <i>Kleinwort Benson v Malaysia Mining</i> (1989).</li>
<li>Promotional gifts: [[c:esso]].</li>
<li>Collective agreements between unions and employers are not binding unless in writing and stated to be (Trade Union and Labour Relations (Consolidation) Act 1992 s179).</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Privity of contract', html: `
<div class="box def"><b class="lbl">The rule</b><p>Only the <b>parties</b> to a contract can sue on it or be bound by it. A third party cannot enforce it, even if it was made for their benefit: [[c:tweddle]]; [[c:dunlopselfridge]].</p></div>
<p>Criticism: it can defeat the parties’ intentions and leave a loss uncompensated — the person with the loss cannot sue, and the person who can sue has no loss. [[c:beswick]] shows the widow only succeeded because she was also her husband’s administratrix.</p>` },
    { h: 'Exceptions to privity', html: `
<ul><li><b>Agency</b>: an agent makes a contract on behalf of a principal, who can sue and be sued.</li>
<li><b>Trusts</b>: a beneficiary under a trust can enforce it.</li>
<li><b>Collateral contracts</b>: [[c:shanklin]].</li>
<li><b>Statute</b>: e.g. third-party motor insurance (Road Traffic Act 1988 s148); Law of Property Act 1925 s56; assignment of rights.</li>
<li><b>Suing for another’s loss</b>: a contracting party may recover damages for a third party’s loss in some situations — family holidays ([[c:jacksonhorizon]]); and the rule in [[c:dunloplambert]]: where it is contemplated that goods (or property) will be transferred to a third party, the original contracting party can recover the third party’s loss for their benefit (extended to building contracts in <i>Linden Gardens Trust v Lenesta Sludge</i>, 1994; limited in <i>Alfred McAlpine v Panatown</i>, 2000, where the third party had its own remedy).</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'The Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999', html: `
<p>Following a Law Commission report (1996), the Act allows a third party to enforce a term if:</p>
<ul><li><b>s1(1)(a)</b> the contract <b>expressly</b> provides that they may; or</li>
<li><b>s1(1)(b)</b> the term <b>purports to confer a benefit</b> on them — unless it appears the parties did not intend it to be enforceable by the third party (s1(2)) — [[c:nisshin]].</li>
<li><b>s1(3)</b>: the third party must be expressly identified by <b>name, class or description</b> (they need not exist when the contract is made — e.g. a future owner).</li>
<li><b>s1(5)</b>: the third party has the same remedies as a party.</li>
<li><b>s2</b>: once the third party has assented or relied, the parties cannot vary or cancel the contract without their consent (unless the contract says otherwise).</li>
<li><b>s3</b>: the promisor can raise the same defences against the third party as against the promisee.</li></ul>
<p>Many commercial contracts expressly exclude the Act, and it does not apply to some contracts (e.g. employment contracts against employees).</p>` }
  ],
  debate: [{ q: 'Has the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 solved the problems of privity?', for: ['Gives effect to the parties’ intentions (e.g. Beswick would now be decided under s1).', 'Clear statutory test; Nisshin shows it working in commercial contracts.', 'Protects third parties who rely on the contract (s2).'], ag: ['Parties routinely contract out of it.', 'The third party must be identified in the contract.', 'Common law exceptions (Dunlop v Lambert, Linden Gardens) remain complex.', 'Uncertainty about when parties “did not intend” enforcement.'] }],
  cases: ['balfour', 'merritt', 'jones', 'simpkins', 'parkerclark', 'rosefrank', 'edwards', 'esso', 'tweddle', 'dunlopselfridge', 'beswick', 'jacksonhorizon', 'dunloplambert', 'shanklin', 'nisshin'],
  worked: [{ q: '<b>Scenario.</b> Mo pays a florist £300 to deliver a wedding bouquet to his sister Nia. The contract says: “Delivery to Nia Jones, the bride.” The bouquet arrives wilted. Advise Nia.', s: ['<b class="st">Privity</b>Nia is not a party to the contract (Tweddle v Atkinson), so at common law she could not sue.', '<b class="st">1999 Act</b>Under s1(1)(b) the delivery term purports to confer a benefit on her, and she is identified by name (s1(3)). Nothing suggests the parties did not intend her to enforce it (s1(2)).', '<b class="st">Remedy</b>She has the same remedies as a party (s1(5)) — damages for breach of the florist’s duty to perform with reasonable care and skill (CRA 2015 s49 applies to Mo as the consumer).', '<b class="st">Alternatively</b>Mo can sue as a party.'], a: 'Nia can enforce the term under the 1999 Act.' }],
  pitfalls: ['Forgetting to explain that presumptions can be rebutted.', 'Saying family agreements can never be binding.', 'Describing privity without the 1999 Act and its sections.', 'Ignoring Dunlop v Lambert, which is named in the specification.'],
  cards: [
    ['Presumption in social and domestic agreements?', 'No intention to create legal relations.'],
    ['Balfour v Balfour (1919)?', 'Agreement between spouses living together not binding.'],
    ['Merritt v Merritt (1970)?', 'Separated spouses’ written agreement binding.'],
    ['Simpkins v Pays (1955)?', 'Shared competition entry — mutuality rebutted the presumption.'],
    ['Presumption in commercial agreements?', 'Intention to create legal relations — strong.'],
    ['Rose and Frank v Crompton (1925)?', 'An honour clause rebutted the commercial presumption.'],
    ['Edwards v Skyways (1964)?', 'An “ex gratia” payment promise was still binding.'],
    ['What is privity?', 'Only parties to a contract can enforce it or be bound by it.'],
    ['Tweddle v Atkinson (1861)?', 'A son could not enforce his father-in-law’s promise to pay him.'],
    ['Dunlop v Lambert (1839)?', 'A contracting party could recover a third party’s loss where goods pass to that third party.'],
    ['CRTPA 1999 s1(1)?', 'A third party can enforce a term if the contract expressly says so or the term purports to benefit them.'],
    ['CRTPA 1999 s1(3)?', 'The third party must be identified by name, class or description.'],
    ['Nisshin Shipping v Cleaves (2003)?', 'Brokers could enforce commission terms under s1(1)(b).']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'In Balfour v Balfour the agreement was not binding because…', o: ['it was a domestic agreement between spouses living together', 'there was no offer', 'consideration was past', 'it was not in writing'], x: 'Presumption against intention.' },
    { q: 'The presumption in commercial agreements is that…', o: ['the parties intend to be legally bound', 'they do not intend to be bound', 'only written contracts are binding', 'honour clauses are void'], x: 'Edwards v Skyways.' },
    { q: 'An “honour clause” was effective in…', o: ['Rose and Frank v Crompton', 'Edwards v Skyways', 'Merritt v Merritt', 'Esso v Commissioners'], x: 'Agreement not legally binding.' },
    { q: 'Tweddle v Atkinson is an authority on…', o: ['privity of contract', 'intention to create legal relations', 'the postal rule', 'frustration'], x: 'The son could not sue.' },
    { q: 'Under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, a third party must be identified…', o: ['by name, class or description', 'by name only', 'by signing the contract', 'by paying consideration'], x: 's1(3).' },
    { q: 'In Beswick v Beswick the widow succeeded because…', o: ['she sued as administratrix of her husband’s estate', 'she was a party to the contract', 'the 1999 Act applied', 'there was an honour clause'], x: 'Specific performance ordered.' },
    { q: 'Which case recognised that a contracting party may recover damages for a third party’s loss where goods pass to the third party?', o: ['Dunlop v Lambert', 'Dunlop v Selfridge', 'Jones v Padavatton', 'Simpkins v Pays'], x: 'Named in the specification.' },
    { q: 'In Merritt v Merritt, the presumption against intention was rebutted mainly because…', o: ['the spouses had separated and the agreement was written', 'money was paid in advance', 'a solicitor was present', 'it concerned a business'], x: 'Distinguished Balfour.' }
  ],
  exam: [
    { q: 'Explain the rules on intention to create legal relations. [10]', m: 10, ms: ['need for intention', 'domestic presumption — Balfour', 'rebutted — Merritt; separation', 'family — Jones v Padavatton', 'social — Simpkins v Pays; Parker v Clark', 'commercial presumption — Edwards v Skyways', 'honour clauses — Rose and Frank', 'Esso / comfort letters'] },
    { q: 'Analyse and evaluate the doctrine of privity of contract. [25]', m: 25, ms: ['rule — Tweddle, Dunlop v Selfridge', 'justifications — freedom, consideration moves from promisee', 'injustice — Beswick; loss/claim gap', 'exceptions — agency, trusts, collateral (Shanklin)', 'Jackson v Horizon', 'Dunlop v Lambert, Linden Gardens, Panatown', 'CRTPA 1999 — s1, s2, s3', 'Nisshin; contracting out', 'remaining gaps', 'conclusion'] }
  ],
  tools: ['intentsort']
});

/* ---------- tools for 2.1.2 ---------- */
TOOLS.offertree = { type: 'tree', title: 'Is there a contract yet?', intro: 'Walk through formation step by step, the way you would in a Component 2 answer.', nodes: {
  start: { q: 'What did the first party do?', o: [['Displayed goods in a shop or on a website', 'itt'], ['Placed an advert promising a reward for an act', 'uni'], ['Stated a price when asked', 'info'], ['Made a definite promise to sell on stated terms', 'offer']] },
  itt: { end: true, tone: 'mid', v: 'Invitation to treat — no offer yet', x: 'The customer makes the offer (at the till or by placing an order); the seller then accepts or refuses.', cases: ['fisherbell', 'boots', 'partridge'] },
  info: { end: true, tone: 'mid', v: 'Supplying information — no offer', x: 'A reply giving a price is not a promise to sell.', cases: ['harvey'] },
  uni: { q: 'Has the other party performed the requested act, knowing of the offer?', o: [['Yes, completely', 'unid'], ['They have started', 'unis'], ['No', 'unin']] },
  unid: { end: true, tone: 'good', v: 'Unilateral contract formed', x: 'Performance is acceptance; no need to notify the offeror.', cases: ['carlill'] },
  unis: { end: true, tone: 'mid', v: 'The offer can no longer be revoked', x: 'Once performance has begun, the offeror cannot revoke.', cases: ['errington'] },
  unin: { end: true, tone: 'mid', v: 'Offer still open — may be revoked', x: 'Revocation of a unilateral offer to the world can be made by the same means used to make it (Shuey v US).' },
  offer: { q: 'Before acceptance, was the offer ended?', o: [['Revoked, and the revocation was received (or learned from a reliable third party)', 'rev'], ['The offeree made a counter-offer', 'counter'], ['A long time passed', 'lapse'], ['No — it was still open', 'acc']] },
  rev: { end: true, tone: 'bad', v: 'No contract — offer revoked', x: 'Revocation is effective when communicated, even via a reliable third party.', cases: ['byrne', 'dickinson', 'routledge'] },
  counter: { end: true, tone: 'bad', v: 'Original offer destroyed', x: 'The counter-offer is a new offer that the original offeror may accept. A mere request for information would not destroy the offer (Stevenson v McLean).', cases: ['hyde'] },
  lapse: { end: true, tone: 'bad', v: 'Offer lapsed', x: 'After the stated time or a reasonable time, depending on the subject matter.' },
  acc: { q: 'Did the offeree accept all the terms unconditionally?', o: [['Yes', 'how'], ['They added new terms', 'counter'], ['They stayed silent', 'silence']] },
  silence: { end: true, tone: 'bad', v: 'No acceptance', x: 'Silence cannot be acceptance.', cases: ['felthouse'] },
  how: { q: 'How was acceptance sent?', o: [['By post, where post was reasonable and not excluded', 'post'], ['By phone, telex or face to face', 'inst'], ['By email or online', 'email']] },
  post: { end: true, tone: 'good', v: 'Contract formed when the letter was posted', x: 'The postal rule applies even if the letter is delayed or lost, unless the offer requires actual receipt.', cases: ['adams', 'householdfire', 'holwell'] },
  inst: { end: true, tone: 'good', v: 'Contract formed when acceptance was received', x: 'Instantaneous communication.', cases: ['entores', 'brinkibon'] },
  email: { end: true, tone: 'good', v: 'Contract probably formed on receipt', x: 'Likely when the email arrives in business hours; websites must acknowledge orders under the E-Commerce Regulations 2002.', cases: ['thomasbpe'] }
} };
TOOLS.formsort = { type: 'sort', title: 'Offer or invitation to treat?', intro: 'Classify each.', cats: ['Offer', 'Invitation to treat', 'Neither'], items: [
  ['A trainer in a shop window with a £90 price tag.', 'Invitation to treat', 'Fisher v Bell.'],
  ['“£50 reward to anyone who returns my lost cat.”', 'Offer', 'Unilateral offer — Carlill.'],
  ['A catalogue listing garden furniture and prices.', 'Invitation to treat', 'Like Partridge v Crittenden.'],
  ['“I will sell you my bike for £120 — let me know by Friday.”', 'Offer', 'Definite promise on specific terms.'],
  ['“Would you be interested in selling your laptop?”', 'Neither', 'An enquiry, not a promise.'],
  ['A bid at an auction.', 'Offer', 'The auctioneer accepts at the fall of the hammer.'],
  ['“Lowest price for the flat: £200,000.” (reply to a question)', 'Neither', 'Harvey v Facey — supply of information.'],
  ['A customer puts a basket of shopping on the checkout.', 'Offer', 'Boots — the customer offers.']
] };
TOOLS.considertree = { type: 'tree', title: 'Is there good consideration?', intro: 'Test a promise for consideration.', nodes: {
  start: { q: 'What did the promisee give in return for the promise?', o: [['Something already done before the promise was made', 'past'], ['Something they were already bound to do', 'exist'], ['Part payment of a debt', 'part'], ['A new act or promise of some value', 'value']] },
  value: { q: 'Does it have some value in the eyes of the law (even if small)?', o: [['Yes — e.g. £1, wrappers, a promise to do work', 'good'], ['No — e.g. a promise to stop complaining, love and affection', 'bad1']] },
  good: { end: true, tone: 'good', v: 'Good consideration', x: 'Consideration must be sufficient but need not be adequate.', cases: ['thomasthomas', 'chappell'] },
  bad1: { end: true, tone: 'bad', v: 'Not sufficient', x: 'Intangible or worthless acts are not consideration.', cases: ['whitebluett'] },
  past: { q: 'Was the act done at the promisor’s request, with payment understood, and would it have been enforceable if promised in advance?', o: [['Yes', 'pastok'], ['No', 'pastbad']] },
  pastok: { end: true, tone: 'good', v: 'Good consideration (exception)', x: 'The Lampleigh v Braithwait / Pao On exception.', cases: ['lampleigh', 'paoon'] },
  pastbad: { end: true, tone: 'bad', v: 'Past consideration — not good', x: 'The act came before the promise.', cases: ['mcardle'] },
  exist: { q: 'Who is the existing duty owed to?', o: [['The public (a legal duty)', 'pub'], ['The promisor, under an existing contract', 'con'], ['A third party', 'third']] },
  pub: { q: 'Did the promisee do more than the duty required?', o: [['Yes', 'pubok'], ['No', 'pubbad']] },
  pubok: { end: true, tone: 'good', v: 'Good consideration', x: 'Exceeding a public duty.', cases: ['glasbrook'] },
  pubbad: { end: true, tone: 'bad', v: 'Not good consideration', x: 'Merely performing a public duty.', cases: ['collins'] },
  con: { q: 'Did they do more than the contract required, or does the promisor get a practical benefit without economic duress?', o: [['Did more than required', 'conmore'], ['Practical benefit, no duress', 'conroffey'], ['Neither', 'conbad']] },
  conmore: { end: true, tone: 'good', v: 'Good consideration', x: 'Exceeding the contractual duty.', cases: ['hartley'] },
  conroffey: { end: true, tone: 'good', v: 'Good consideration (practical benefit)', x: 'But check for economic duress.', cases: ['roffey'] },
  conbad: { end: true, tone: 'bad', v: 'Not good consideration', x: 'Performing an existing duty owed to the promisor.', cases: ['stilk'] },
  third: { end: true, tone: 'good', v: 'Good consideration', x: 'Performing a duty owed to a third party is good consideration (Shadwell v Shadwell; Scotson v Pegg).' },
  part: { q: 'Is there anything extra — early payment, a different place at the creditor’s request, something different in kind, payment by a third party?', o: [['Yes', 'partok'], ['No — but the creditor promised and the debtor relied on it', 'pe'], ['No', 'partbad']] },
  partok: { end: true, tone: 'good', v: 'Debt discharged', x: 'An exception to Pinnel’s Case.' },
  partbad: { end: true, tone: 'bad', v: 'Creditor can claim the balance', x: 'Practical benefit does not apply to part payment (Re Selectmove).', cases: ['foakes', 'selectmove'] },
  pe: { q: 'Did the debtor act equitably (no pressure or exploitation)?', o: [['Yes', 'peok'], ['No', 'pebad']] },
  peok: { end: true, tone: 'mid', v: 'Promissory estoppel may protect the debtor', x: 'A shield, not a sword; may only suspend the creditor’s rights.', cases: ['hightrees', 'combe'] },
  pebad: { end: true, tone: 'bad', v: 'No estoppel', x: 'Equity will not help a party who exploited the other.', cases: ['dcbuilders'] }
} };
